    "For internet-exposed Moltbot instances, the report implies that zero-trust is the right mitigation model because the platform’s default assumptions are unsafe: it trusts localhost, stores secrets in plaintext, exposes admin surfaces, and allows third-party skills with full system access.",
    "The main zero-trust mitigations include treating every request as untrusted, removing direct public exposure, enforcing strong identity and session controls, segmenting and isolating the platform, moving secrets out of plaintext local storage, constraining third-party skills, controlling outbound communication, hardening updates and the supply chain, and monitoring for compromise.",
    "The report says not to trust localhost, reverse proxies, tunnels, or internal network location.",
    "The report says every admin/API/WebSocket action should require explicit authentication and authorization.",
    "The report says least privilege should be enforced for all users, services, and skills.",
    "The report recommends putting Moltbot behind a zero-trust access layer such as an identity-aware proxy, a VPN/ZTNA gateway, or an mTLS-authenticated reverse proxy.",
    "The report says public access should be blocked to admin dashboards, WebSocket endpoints, and command execution interfaces.",
    "The report says all administrative access should require MFA.",
    "The report says short-lived sessions, re-authentication for sensitive actions, and session revocation should be used.",
    "The report says trust based on IP or network segment alone should be eliminated.",
    "The report recommends running Moltbot in a container or VM sandbox with minimal host permissions.",
    "The report recommends filesystem isolation so compromise of a skill cannot read all local secrets.",
    "The report says the app runtime, credential store, secrets manager, and logging/telemetry should be separated.",
    "The report says broad read/write access to user files and host directories should be denied.",
    "The report says plaintext Markdown/JSON credential storage should be replaced with a secrets manager or encrypted vault.",
    "The report says per-service scoped credentials should be used and rotated regularly.",
